In this Continual-ARC instance, eight demonstrations varied dramatically in dimensions (from 10x6 to 29x30), palette, background, edge-bar placement, sparse marker locations, and output deltas. Some demos extended a line from a marker, others filled regions, and others changed differently shaped areas. The test was a 14x11 grid with a 3-cell color-2 bar at the top and bottom, plus three isolated color-3 cells.

The outputs did not support a single obvious selector/operation by visual analogy. Despite that, multiple unsupported candidates were submitted: extend a marker-to-edge ray, replace marker color, recolor bars, stamp/paint motif regions, and fill regions. They were all rejected. The reusable lesson is to maintain an exact delta ledger and require a deterministic rule that replays every pair before transfer. Extreme demo heterogeneity and repeated wrong feedback should increase caution, not prompt cosmetic variants. Do not assume that markers become color 4 or that edge bars imply rays; those features appeared in distinct, incompatible demos. Following wrong feedback, retire the entire candidate and reassess task class/output mapping. When no rule can be validated, avoid cycling through speculative guesses; honor action protocol while being explicit internally about uncertainty.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u043/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-stream-demo-discipline/references/task-36d8805c-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/marker-anchor-offsets.md", "content": "# Marker-to-template anchor offsets

When demos show sparse markers receiving copies of an embedded motif, keep two coordinate concepts separate: (1) the motif's bounding-box origin and (2) the motif coordinate that aligns with a marker. Represent the latter as a signed offset from the origin and validate it against multiple marker/copy pairs. A plausible colored pattern is not enough: replay every marker footprint against the complete output, including the original motif, overlaps, clipping, and unchanged candidate markers.

A repeated failure mode is to shift the right motif by an intuitively appealing amount (top-left, center, a nearby marker, or a visually salient source pixel) without validating the anchor. On incorrect feedback, discard that placement hypothesis and derive the offset anew from observed coordinate correspondences. Also validate serialization independently: every row must have the expected width and all values must be legal before submitting.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u043/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/marker-anchor-offsets.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-demo-discipline", "file": "references/grid-serialization-validation.md", "content": "# Grid serialization check

A visually copied or manually assembled output can be invalid even when the task rule is understood. Before submission, verify mechanically that the output has exactly the expected number of rows and that each row has exactly the expected number of cells. Check values are integers in the allowed range, then serialize the same validated grid. Do not infer successful dimensionality from the visual layout: inspect all row lengths, since one omitted background cell in a row is enough to make a nominal 19x17 output malformed.
